  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Deferred Section 1031 exchanges impose two strict deadlines from the transfer of the relinquished property: (1) the replacement must be identified within 45 days, and (2) closing must occur within 180 days (or the tax return due date, whichever is earlier). Patel's March 1 transfer sets the 45-day deadline at April 15. The April 20 identification attempt is 5 days late and is completely invalid — there is no extension, modification, or penalty option. If Patel cannot acquire one of the three timely-identified properties, the exchange fails entirely, and the transaction is treated as a taxable sale. Sharma met both deadlines: identified by April 10 (40 days) and closed by July 15 (136 days). These strict deadlines prevent taxpayers from using open-ended exchanges as indefinite deferral vehicles. (A) is wrong because the 45-day deadline is absolute with no modification period. (B) is wrong because closing has a separate 180-day window. (D) is wrong because there is no penalty option — late identification simply invalidates the exchange.$EXPL$,
  stem = $EXPL$Patel transfers relinquished property on March 1 in a deferred exchange. Patel identifies three replacement properties by April 14 but cannot close on any of them by the 45-day deadline. Patel's CPA suggests identifying a fourth property on April 20. Meanwhile, Patel's colleague, Sharma, transferred property on the same date and identified one replacement by April 10, closing on July 15. How do the two exchange timelines compare, and what is the consequence of Patel's late identification?$EXPL$
WHERE id = 220;

-- MANUAL REVIEW: Q221 — Label changed from A to D — verify explanation reasoning matches choice D
-- Q221: Fix #6
