  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): When a taxpayer is relieved of a mortgage in a Section 1031 exchange, the relief is treated as boot received (equivalent to receiving cash). Evans's $100,000 mortgage relief is boot, and the recognized gain is $100,000 (limited by the realized gain of $200,000). If Evans also assumed a $60,000 mortgage on the warehouse, liabilities are netted: $100,000 relief − $60,000 assumed = $40,000 net boot, reducing recognized gain to $40,000. The netting principle reflects economic reality — Evans's true cash-equivalent extraction from the exchange is the net change in indebtedness. (B) is wrong because mortgage involvement does not disqualify the exchange; it merely triggers partial gain recognition. (C) is wrong because mortgage relief has been treated as boot since the regulations were established. (D) is wrong because assumed liabilities on replacement property directly reduce net boot — this netting is fundamental to Section 1031 calculations.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 221;

