  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): IRC Section 1378 requires S corporations to use a calendar year unless the corporation establishes a valid business purpose for a different year-end (a difficult standard to meet) or elects under Section 444 with a required payment. The rationale is anti-deferral: individual shareholders file on a calendar year, so allowing a September 30 fiscal year would mean income earned October 1 through December 31 would not appear on the shareholder's K-1 until the following year — up to a 15-month deferral for income earned in October. This systemic deferral advantage was considered unfair compared to self-employed individuals and partners in calendar-year partnerships. (B) is wrong because fiscal years create real timing mismatches — the pass-through rules do not automatically solve this. (C) is wrong because there is no September 30 requirement, and estimated payments are not the basis for the rule. (D) is wrong because S corporations default to calendar year, not the majority shareholder's year.$EXPL$
