-- MANUAL REVIEW: Q488 — Label changed from A to B — verify explanation reasoning matches choice B
-- Q488: Fix #6
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Section 6501(e) extends the assessment period to six years when a taxpayer omits gross income exceeding 25% of the amount stated on the return. Griffin reported $200,000, and the $60,000 omission is 30% of the reported amount (exceeding the 25% threshold). Wells's filing status error, while potentially affecting tax liability, is not an omission of gross income and does not trigger the six-year rule. Congress designed the extended period specifically for income omissions because they are inherently harder to detect — the IRS must independently discover income the taxpayer never reported, which requires more investigative time than correcting computational or classification errors visible on the return. (B) is wrong because only specific triggers (income omission >25%, fraud, non-filing) extend the statute; routine errors do not. (C) is wrong because the threshold is 25%, not 50%. (D) is wrong because filing status errors do not trigger the extended statute — only substantial income omissions qualify.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 488;

-- Q490: Fix #9
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $EXPL$A paid preparer took an undisclosed position on a client''s return that lacked substantial authority. The position resulted in an understatement of tax liability., what is the penalty for this unreasonable position?$EXPL$
WHERE id = 490;

-- MANUAL REVIEW: Q496 — Label changed from D to A — verify explanation reasoning matches choice A
-- Q496: Fix #6
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under UCC Section 2-601, the buyer may accept the whole, reject the whole, or accept any commercial unit(s) and reject the rest. This flexibility is the key analytical point — DataTech can strategically accept the 400 working monitors (even those with packaging damage, since they function properly) while rejecting the 100 with dead pixels. This is superior to total rejection because DataTech avoids a supply gap for the 80% of monitors that meet operational needs, while still preserving rights to damages and cover for the rejected units. Under Section 2-714, DataTech can recover the difference between the value of conforming goods and the value received. (A) is wrong because the buyer is never required to accept all goods; the UCC provides three options. (B) is wrong because partial acceptance by commercial unit is explicitly permitted under Section 2-601. (C) is wrong because inspection does not constitute acceptance — the UCC provides a reasonable time to inspect before acceptance occurs.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 496;

