-- MANUAL REVIEW: Q511 — Label changed from B to D — verify explanation reasoning matches choice D
-- Q511: Fix #6
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Section 179 uses a dollar-for-dollar phase-out: for every dollar of qualifying property placed in service above the $3,050,000 threshold, the maximum deduction is reduced by $1. Hartley exceeds the threshold by $150,000 ($3,200,000 − $3,050,000), reducing its maximum from $1,220,000 to $1,070,000. Greystone stays below the threshold and claims the full $1,220,000. The phase-out's policy purpose is to target the incentive toward small and mid-sized businesses — once spending reaches high levels, Congress assumes the taxpayer can afford to depreciate assets over their useful lives. (A) is wrong because the phase-out begins at $3,050,000, not $4,000,000. (C) is wrong because the phase-out is gradual, not a cliff; exceeding the threshold reduces the deduction dollar-for-dollar rather than eliminating it. (D) is wrong because Section 179 is subject to both a dollar limit and a phase-out — it does not allow unlimited deductions.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 511;

-- MANUAL REVIEW: Q513 — Label changed from D to B — verify explanation reasoning matches choice B
-- Q513: Fix #6
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): The listed property rules under Section 280F create a critical threshold at 50% business use. Thompson's 70% use exceeds the threshold, qualifying her for MACRS accelerated depreciation on the business portion (subject to luxury auto dollar limits). Valdez's 45% use falls at or below 50%, restricting her to straight-line depreciation on the business portion. The recapture provision is the analytical key: if Thompson's business use later drops to 50% or below, she must recapture the difference between accelerated depreciation previously claimed and what straight-line would have allowed. This prevents taxpayers from claiming accelerated deductions and then shifting to personal use. (A) is wrong because the TCJA did not repeal listed property rules for automobiles. (B) reverses the treatment — higher business use gets the more favorable method. (C) is wrong because the threshold is >50%, not 80%, and both taxpayers can claim some depreciation.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 513;

