-- MANUAL REVIEW: Q551 — Label changed from A to D — verify explanation reasoning matches choice D
-- Q551: Fix #6
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under Section 469(g), when a taxpayer disposes of an entire interest in a passive activity in a fully taxable transaction, all accumulated suspended passive losses are released and may offset any type of income (active, portfolio, or passive). Kowalski's $120,000 in suspended losses would be fully deductible, netting against the $30,000 gain and producing a net $90,000 deduction against other income. However, in a gift, the suspended losses are not released to the donor — instead, they increase the donee's basis in the property under Section 469(j)(6). This distinction is critical for planning because a gift permanently denies the donor the ability to use those suspended losses. (B) is wrong because the complete disposition rule specifically allows deduction of suspended losses. (C) is wrong because the full amount is released in a taxable sale, not just the gain amount. (D) is wrong because gifts and other nontaxable transfers do not trigger loss release — only fully taxable dispositions qualify.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 551;

