-- Q663: Fix #9
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $EXPL$Pinnacle Software sells a three-element bundle for $300,000: a software license (SSP $180,000), implementation services (SSP $90,000), and a 2-year support contract (SSP $30,000). After a pricing study, Pinnacle revises the SSP for implementation services from $90,000 to $150,000. Analyze how this SSP revision shifts revenue allocation among the performance obligations for new contracts, and determine whether Pinnacle must restate prior period contracts$EXPL$,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under ASC 606-10-32-33, the transaction price is allocated to performance obligations based on relative standalone selling prices at contract inception. With the revised SSPs ($180,000 + $150,000 + $30,000 = $360,000 pool), the allocation shifts: software license receives $150,000 ($180K/$360K x $300K = 50%), implementation receives $125,000 ($150K/$360K x $300K = 41.7%), and support receives $25,000 ($30K/$360K x $300K = 8.3%). Critically, prior period contracts are not restated — ASC 606 uses the SSPs available at the time the original contract was executed. The revised SSPs apply only to contracts entered into after the pricing study. This protects previously recognized revenue from retroactive adjustment while ensuring new contracts reflect current market pricing. (A) is wrong because ASC 606 does not require retroactive restatement for SSP changes. (C) is wrong because SSPs must be updated to reflect current observable evidence. (D) is wrong because the transaction price ($300,000) is determined by the contract terms, not by SSPs — SSPs only determine how the fixed transaction price is allocated. (See ASC 606..)$EXPL$
WHERE id = 663;

