-- Q1078: Fix #9
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $EXPL$In Scenario A, Carver Corp. modifies a lease to add a second floor of office space at a standalone price, increasing total consideration proportionally. In Scenario B, Carver modifies a lease to extend the term by 3 years on the existing space without adding new scope, and the revised payments are below market rates. Analyze why treats these two modifications differently, and compare the effect on the right-of-use asset.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1078;

-- Q1079: Fix #9
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $EXPL$In Scenario A, Dalton Manufacturing sells a factory with a carrying value of $500,000 and a fair value of $750,000 for exactly $750,000, then leases it back at market rates. In Scenario B, Dalton sells the identical factory for $850,000 (above fair value) and leases it back at above-market rental rates. Both transfers qualify as sales Analyze the gain recognition pattern in each scenario and explain how treats the $100,000 excess above fair value in Scenario B.$EXPL$,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under ASC 842-40-25-4, when the sale price equals fair value, the seller-lessee recognizes the full gain on the difference between carrying amount and fair value. In Scenario A: $750,000 - $500,000 = $250,000 gain recognized immediately. When the sale price exceeds fair value (Scenario B), ASC 842-40-30-1 requires bifurcation: the $250,000 representing the genuine fair-value sale is recognized as a gain, while the $100,000 excess ($850,000 - $750,000) is accounted for as additional financing — effectively a loan from the buyer-lessor repaid through the above-market lease payments. The $100,000 is recorded as a financial liability and reduced over the lease term as the above-market rent payments are made. This prevents seller-lessees from inflating sale gains by negotiating above-market sale prices offset by above-market lease payments. (B) is wrong because ASC 842 does not permit recognition of the excess above fair value as a gain. (C) is wrong because ASC 842 generally permits immediate gain recognition at fair value, unlike the old ASC 840. (D) is wrong because a fair-value sale does produce a gain equal to the FV minus carrying amount. (See ASC 842..)$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1079;

