-- Q1086: Fix #9
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $EXPL$Clearwater Industries' pension plan has a projected benefit obligation (PBO) of $12,000,000 and plan assets of $10,500,000, producing a funded status liability of $1,500,000. An actuarial loss of $500,000 occurs due to lower-than-expected investment returns. Analyze how this loss affects the funded status on the balance sheet and explain why uses OCI as an intermediary rather than recognizing the full $500,000 immediately in pension expense.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1086;

-- Q1088: Fix #9
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $EXPL$Harrington Consulting sponsors a defined contribution plan (401(k) with 4% match), while its competitor Kelton Advisory sponsors a defined benefit plan promising 1.5% of final average salary per year of service. Both employers spend approximately $500,000 annually on retirement benefits. Analyze why the defined contribution plan creates straightforward expense recognition while the defined benefit plan requires complex actuarial calculations and balance sheet recognition$EXPL$,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under ASC 715, the fundamental distinction is who bears the risk. In Harrington's defined contribution plan, the employer's obligation is limited to the annual contribution. Once made, no further liability exists — investment risk, longevity risk, and inflation risk transfer to the employee. Expense recognition is simply the contribution amount in each period. Kelton's defined benefit plan promises a specific retirement benefit (1.5% * years * final salary), meaning the employer bears all actuarial risks. ASC 715 requires estimating the PBO using assumptions about discount rates, salary growth, employee turnover, and mortality. The funded status (PBO minus plan assets) appears on the balance sheet, and pension expense has multiple components: service cost, interest cost, expected return on plan assets, and amortization of prior service costs and actuarial gains/losses. This complexity arises because the employer's economic obligation extends decades into the future and depends on uncertain variables. Choice A ignores the fundamental accounting distinction. Choice C reverses the complexity — individual account tracking is administrative, not accounting complexity. Choice D reverses the relationship — the benefit promise creates estimation uncertainty, not certainty. (See ASC 715..)$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1088;

