-- Q3188: Fix #9
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $EXPL$Kensington Corp. designated a derivative as a cash flow hedge that has been effective for two years. At the end of Q2, the hedge fails the effectiveness test At that point, $120,000 of gains are accumulated in OCI from prior periods. Analyze the effect of the hedge disqualification on Kensington's balance sheet and income statement, and explain what happens to the $120,000 already in OCI.$EXPL$,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under ASC 815, when a cash flow hedge fails the effectiveness test, the previously accumulated gains or losses in OCI are not immediately dumped into earnings. The $120,000 remains in accumulated OCI and follows the normal reclassification pattern — it is reclassified to earnings in the period(s) when the hedged forecasted transaction affects earnings. However, if the forecasted transaction is no longer probable, the amount in OCI must be reclassified to earnings immediately. Prospectively, the derivative is no longer designated as a hedge, so it remains on the balance sheet at fair value with all subsequent fair value changes recognized in earnings. This creates a bifurcated treatment: legacy OCI follows the hedged transaction, while new changes hit earnings. (A) is wrong because the $120,000 is not immediately reclassified unless the forecasted transaction is no longer probable. (B) is wrong because a hedge that was previously effective is not an error — the disqualification is prospective. (D) is wrong because derivatives are always carried at fair value on the balance sheet regardless of hedge designation. (See ASC 815..)$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3188;

