  stem = $EXPL$Pryor Manufacturing sells a factory building with a carrying value of $2.4 million to Weldon Capital for $3.5 million and immediately leases it back for seven years. The transfer qualifies as a sale As the seller-lessee, Pryor should recognize:$EXPL$,
  explanation = $EXPL$Under ASC 842, the seller-lessee recognizes a gain or loss only on the portion of the asset that is effectively transferred (not retained through the leaseback). The gain related to the right-of-use asset retained is deferred. If the sale price and lease payments are at market, the full gain may be recognized. (See ASC 606..)$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3269;

-- Q3271: Fix #9
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $EXPL$Waverly Corp. enters a 10-month equipment lease with monthly payments of $5,000. The company is deciding whether to elect the short-term lease exemption Analyze the trade-offs of electing vs. not electing the exemption, and compare the balance sheet and income statement effects under each approach. Explain when the exemption is advantageous.$EXPL$,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under ASC 842-20-25-2, a lessee may elect (by class of underlying asset) to exclude short-term leases (12 months or less with no purchase option the lessee is reasonably certain to exercise) from balance sheet recognition. Without the election, the 10-month lease requires recognizing an ROU asset and lease liability based on the present value of the remaining payments (approximately $48,000 depending on the discount rate), with an amortization-plus-interest expense pattern. With the election, no asset or liability is recognized and the $5,000 monthly payments are straight-line rent expense. The exemption is advantageous when: (1) tracking and measuring short-term leases imposes disproportionate administrative burden, (2) the company prefers lower reported liabilities for covenant or optics reasons, and (3) the portfolio of short-term leases is immaterial. Total expense over 10 months is approximately $50,000 under both approaches, but the pattern and balance sheet presentation differ materially. (A) is wrong because avoiding liability recognition can be a strategic advantage. (C) is wrong because the exemption has no tax penalty. (D) is wrong because the balance sheet treatment is fundamentally different with vs. without the exemption. (See ASC 842..)$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3271;

