-- Q4479: Fix #6, #9
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Wrong (A): Under AU-C 210.A21-.A22, when management imposes a scope limitation on a required audit, the auditor should first communicate with those charged with governance (here, the full board) to attempt to resolve the limitation. The auditor must then evaluate whether it is possible to perform the audit in accordance with professional standards despite the restriction.
Wrong (B): The auditor cannot simply accept a management-imposed scope limitation and work around it. The limitation must be evaluated for its effect on the ability to form an opinion, and governance must be informed.
Wrong (C): An immediate disclaimer is premature. The auditor should first attempt to resolve the limitation through communication with governance before concluding the engagement cannot be performed.
Correct (D): An emphasis-of-matter paragraph is used for matters appropriately presented in the financial statements, not to disclose scope limitations. A scope limitation would require a qualified opinion or disclaimer, not an EOM paragraph.$EXPL$,
  stem = $EXPL$Whitfield & Associates LLP has been engaged to audit Lakeshore Municipal Authority, whose enabling legislation requires an annual audit. During planning, the executive director informs the engagement partner that the authority will not provide access to minutes from closed-session board meetings, citing confidentiality., how should the auditor respond to this management-imposed scope limitation?$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4479;

