-- Q7785: Fix #9
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $EXPL$In 2026, 70-year-old Margaret Townsend passes away with a $300,000 traditional IRA. Her sole beneficiary is her 45-year-old son Brian, who has been certified disabled 7). Margaret had already begun taking RMDs. Under the SECURE Act, how must Brian distribute the inherited IRA?$EXPL$,
  explanation = $EXPL$Under IRC Section 401(a)(9)(E)(ii), a disabled beneficiary qualifies as an eligible designated beneficiary and is exempt from the SECURE Act's 10-year rule. Brian may take annual RMDs stretched over his own single life expectancy, preserving tax deferral. Choice B is incorrect because the 10-year rule applies only to non-eligible designated beneficiaries, whereas disabled individuals qualify for the life-expectancy stretch exception. (See Section 72.)$EXPL$
WHERE id = 7785;

-- Q7855: Fix #9
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $EXPL$Brightwater Corp. engaged a CPA to provide assurance on its financial forecast for a bank loan application. The forecast is based on management's expected conditions and will be distributed to multiple potential lenders. Under AT-C, which type of engagement is most appropriate?$EXPL$,
  explanation = $EXPL$Under AT-C 305, a financial forecast for general distribution may be examined, providing positive assurance (an opinion) that the assumptions are reasonable and the presentation conforms to AICPA guidelines. This gives the broadest assurance appropriate for multiple users. Choice D is incorrect because CPAs cannot audit prospective financial statements — the forward-looking nature of forecasts precludes the level of evidence required for an audit opinion. (See Section 305.)$EXPL$
WHERE id = 7855;

