-- Q10066: Fix #9
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $EXPL$Redstone Holdings issues 5,000 shares of mandatorily redeemable preferred stock classified as a liability Each share has a $100 par value and pays an 8% annual dividend. Net income is $950,000 with 200,000 common shares outstanding. Analyze how the mandatorily redeemable preferred dividends affect the basic EPS numerator.$EXPL$,
  explanation = $EXPL$Under ASC 260-10-45-11A, dividends on mandatorily redeemable preferred stock classified as liabilities under ASC 480 are recognized as interest expense in the income statement, not as equity distributions. Therefore, they already reduce net income and are not separately deducted in the EPS numerator. Basic EPS = $950,000 / 200,000 = $4.75. However, choice A incorrectly double-counts the preferred dividends by deducting them again from net income that already reflects the interest expense. (See ASC 480..)$EXPL$
WHERE id = 10066;

-- Q10235: Fix #9
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $EXPL$Greystone Corp. has entered a forward purchase contract obligating it to repurchase 20,000 shares of its own stock at $50 per share in 18 months. The average market price during the period is $40 per share. The company has net income of $600,000 and 200,000 weighted-average common shares. The CFO contends the contract should be excluded from diluted EPS because it is a derivative. Evaluate whether the CFO's position is correct$EXPL$,
  explanation = $EXPL$Under ASC 260-10-45-35, forward purchase contracts on an entity's own shares are evaluated using the reverse treasury stock method. Because the $50 repurchase price exceeds the $40 market price, the entity effectively overpays. The $1,000,000 outflow could purchase 25,000 shares at market but acquires only 20,000, producing 5,000 incremental dilutive shares. Choice D is incorrect because although share repurchases typically reduce the denominator, the above-market price in a forward contract creates net dilution rather than accretion. (See ASC 260..)$EXPL$
WHERE id = 10235;

