-- Q11310: Fix #9
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $EXPL$Garza invested $300,000 in an oil and gas partnership during 2026. The partnership elected to expense $250,000 in intangible drilling costs (IDCs) . The amortized amount over 10 years would be $25,000. Under what circumstances do the excess IDCs become a tax preference item for AMT ?$EXPL$,
  explanation = $EXPL$Under IRC Section 57(a)(2), excess intangible drilling costs are a tax preference item when they exceed 65% of the taxpayer's net income from the oil and gas property. The excess is the amount by which IDCs expensed exceed what would have been deductible using 10-year amortization. Unlike choice A suggests, the preference applies only when the 65% threshold is exceeded, not automatically on all IDCs. (See Section 263.)$EXPL$
WHERE id = 11310;

-- Q11312: Fix #9
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $EXPL$Thornton owns a working interest in a gold mine. For 2026, his percentage depletion deduction is $80,000, while the property's adjusted basis at year-end is $55,000. Cost depletion would have been $30,000. Analyze the AMT tax preference item arising from Thornton's depletion deduction.$EXPL$,
  explanation = $EXPL$Under IRC Section 57(a)(1), the tax preference for depletion is the excess of the percentage depletion deduction over the adjusted basis of the property at year-end. Here, $80,000 percentage depletion minus $55,000 adjusted basis equals $25,000. Choice A incorrectly compares percentage depletion to cost depletion rather than to the property's adjusted basis, which is the statutory measure. (See Section 613.)$EXPL$
WHERE id = 11312;

