-- Q11501: Fix #9
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $EXPL$Whitfield Industries prepares tax-basis financial statements and manufactures custom industrial components. Under GAAP, the company uses absorption costing with fixed and variable overhead. The controller asks how tax-basis inventory costing differs. Under the tax basis, how does the UNICAP requirement affect inventory measurement?$EXPL$
WHERE id = 11501;

-- Q11503: Fix #9
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $EXPL$Thornberry Corporation converted from GAAP to tax-basis financial statements. The CFO identifies four accounts that will change: (1) ROU assets and lease liabilities will be removed, (2) the CECL allowance will be eliminated, (3) deferred tax assets and liabilities will no longer be recognized, and (4) goodwill impairment testing will be replaced with amortization. Evaluate which combination correctly reflects all four changes under the tax basis.$EXPL$,
  explanation = $EXPL$The tax basis differs from GAAP on all four points: (1) tax basis generally does not recognize ROU assets or lease liabilities per ASC 842; (2) bad debts use direct write-off under IRC Section 166 rather than the CECL allowance; (3) deferred tax assets and liabilities are unnecessary because the financial statements already reflect tax rules; (4) goodwill is amortized over 15 years under IRC Section 197 rather than tested for impairment under ASC 350. (See ASC 326.)$EXPL$
WHERE id = 11503;

-- Q11504: Fix #9
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $EXPL$Lakeview Ventures engages an auditor to report on financial statements prepared under a contractual basis specified in a joint venture agreement. The statements will be used only by the joint venture partners and their legal counsel. Under AU-C, what must the auditor's report include regarding distribution?$EXPL$
WHERE id = 11504;

-- Q11506: Fix #9
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $EXPL$Garrison Manufacturing prepares tax-basis financial statements. The company has $250,000 in temporary differences between GAAP book depreciation and tax depreciation that would create a deferred tax liability The controller asks whether to record the deferred tax liability on the tax-basis balance sheet. Apply the tax-basis framework rules to this situation.$EXPL$,
  explanation = $EXPL$Under the tax basis, financial statements already use IRC recognition and measurement rules. Since both the financial statements and the tax return follow the same rules, there are no temporary differences between book and tax amounts — the concept of deferred tax assets and liabilities from ASC 740 does not apply. When both use the same tax rules, there is nothing to defer. (See ASC 740..)$EXPL$
WHERE id = 11506;

