-- Q11728: Fix #9
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $EXPL$Whitfield Electronics is preparing its December 31, 2025, financial statements and is reviewing several pending matters with outside counsel. The controller must identify items requiring evaluation Which of the following is an example of a loss contingency under this guidance?$EXPL$,
  explanation = $EXPL$Under ASC 450-20-05, a loss contingency involves an existing condition with uncertainty about a possible loss resolved by future events. The threat of government expropriation creates this uncertainty. Choice C is a purchase commitment, not a contingency by itself—it becomes a contingency only if market prices decline below the contract price. Scheduled maintenance and amortization are deterministic obligations with known amounts and timing, not contingencies involving uncertainty about future outcomes. (See ASC 450..)$EXPL$
WHERE id = 11728;

-- Q11730: Fix #9
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $EXPL$Meridian Capital holds a portfolio of mortgage-backed securities previously classified as Level 2 During Q4 2025, trading volume drops 90%, bid-ask spreads triple, and broker quotes become indicative rather than executable. Meridian begins relying on its internal cash flow model with management-developed prepayment and default assumptions. How should Meridian account for this change?$EXPL$,
  explanation = $EXPL$ASC 820-10-35-54 requires classification based on the lowest level of significant input currently used. When observable inputs become unavailable and management substitutes unobservable assumptions, the measurement transfers to Level 3, with required disclosure of amounts and reasons. Retaining at Level 2 is incorrect because classification follows current inputs, not historical ones. (See ASC 820..)$EXPL$
WHERE id = 11730;

