-- Q12297: Fix #9
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $EXPL$Harmon Industries reports pre-tax income of $300,000 in Q1 2025. The estimated annual effective tax rate is 28%, but a $15,000 discrete item (a tax credit from a fixed asset placed in service in Q1) is also recognized. and, what is Harmon's total income tax expense for Q1?$EXPL$,
  explanation = $EXPL$Under ASC 740-270-30-2, interim income tax expense is computed by applying the estimated annual effective tax rate to year-to-date ordinary income, then adjusting for discrete items. Tax on ordinary income: $300,000 × 28% = $84,000. The $15,000 discrete tax credit reduces Q1 expense to $69,000. However, choice A ($84,000) is incorrect because it omits the discrete credit, which must be recognized entirely in the quarter in which it arises. (See ASC 270-10-45.)$EXPL$
WHERE id = 12297;

-- Q12303: Fix #9
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $EXPL$Riverton Group acquired Beacon Corp. in Year 1 for $3,000,000 plus contingent consideration of up to $600,000 based on Year 2 earnings targets. At acquisition, the contingent liability was measured at $400,000 fair value. In Year 2, Beacon meets the target and Riverton pays $550,000., evaluate the cash flow classification of the $550,000 payment, considering the original $400,000 acquisition-date fair value.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 12303;

-- Q12305: Fix #9
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $EXPL$Kingsley Manufacturing enters a cash flow hedge using an interest rate swap to hedge variable-rate debt payments. During Year 2, Kingsley makes $85,000 in net swap settlement payments. The CFO classifies all swap payments as operating activities because they relate to interest expense. The treasurer argues the payments should follow the hedged item's classification. Evaluate the proper classification$EXPL$,
  explanation = $EXPL$Under ASC 230-10-45-27A, cash flows from derivative instruments that are designated as hedges are classified in the same category as the cash flows from the hedged item. Since interest payments on debt are operating activities under U.S. GAAP, the swap settlement payments are also classified as operating. The CFO's conclusion is correct but the reasoning is incomplete—it is not a default rule but the hedged-item-matching principle. Choice B is incorrect because while the debt itself is financing, the interest payments (the hedged cash flows) are operating. (See ASC 230..)$EXPL$
WHERE id = 12305;

-- Q12307: Fix #9
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $EXPL$Pemberton Industries holds company-owned life insurance (COLI) policies on key executives. During Year 2, one executive passes away and Pemberton receives a $750,000 death benefit. The policy had a cash surrender value of $290,000 at the date of death. The $460,000 excess over CSV is reported as other income. Analyze the proper cash flow classification of the $750,000 proceeds$EXPL$,
  explanation = $EXPL$Under ASC 230-10-45-12(d), proceeds from insurance settlements are classified based on the nature of the related item. COLI policies are investment assets, so the entire $750,000 death benefit is classified as an investing inflow. Under the indirect method, the $460,000 gain included in net income would be subtracted from operating activities to avoid double-counting. Choice B incorrectly splits the proceeds; ASC 230 classifies the full insurance proceeds as investing regardless of the CSV/gain breakdown. (See ASC 230..)$EXPL$
WHERE id = 12307;

-- Q12310: Fix #9
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $EXPL$In March 2025, several employees of Orion Pharmaceuticals exercise vested stock options, paying $175,000 in cash to the company., how is this cash received classified on the statement of cash flows?$EXPL$
WHERE id = 12310;

-- Q12311: Fix #9
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $EXPL$Dunmore Textiles has $600,000 in accounts receivable. In Scenario A, Dunmore factors the receivables without recourse for $570,000, qualifying as a sale In Scenario B, Dunmore factors with full recourse and retains substantially all risks, qualifying as a secured borrowing. The CFO wants to understand how each scenario affects the statement of cash flows. Evaluate the classification differences between the two scenarios.$EXPL$,
  explanation = $EXPL$Under ASC 860 and ASC 230, the classification depends on whether the transfer qualifies as a sale. In Scenario A (without recourse, qualifying as a sale), the $570,000 is an operating cash inflow because the receivables are derecognized and the cash replaces the normal collection cycle. In Scenario B (with recourse, secured borrowing), the $570,000 is a financing inflow because the receivables remain on the balance sheet and the advance is effectively a loan. Choice D incorrectly treats both as financing; only the secured borrowing scenario is financing. (See ASC 860..)$EXPL$
WHERE id = 12311;

