-- Q12322: Fix #9
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $EXPL$Langford Corp. sells a manufacturing facility with a book value of $1,200,000 for $1,500,000 and simultaneously leases it back under an operating lease qualifying as a sale, what amount does Langford report as an investing inflow?$EXPL$,
  explanation = $EXPL$Under ASC 230-10-45-12 and ASC 842, when a sale-leaseback qualifies as a sale, the full $1,500,000 cash received is reported as an investing inflow. The $300,000 gain is included in net income and subtracted in the operating section under the indirect method to avoid double-counting. Choice A incorrectly limits the investing inflow to book value; the full cash proceeds, not the book value, are reported as the investing inflow. (See ASC 842..)$EXPL$
WHERE id = 12322;

-- Q12324: Fix #9
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $EXPL$Whitman Development places $350,000 into an escrow account as part of a real estate acquisition that is expected to close in 90 days. The funds are held by a third-party escrow agent and are restricted for the purchase., how should Whitman classify this cash outflow?$EXPL$
WHERE id = 12324;

-- Q12326: Fix #9
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $EXPL$Merritt Construction capitalizes $95,000 of interest on a self-constructed warehouse during Year 2. Total interest paid during the year is $280,000 (including the $95,000 capitalized). The CFO reports $185,000 as an operating outflow and $95,000 as an investing outflow. Analyze whether this classification is appropriate$EXPL$,
  explanation = $EXPL$Under ASC 230-10-45-17(b), all interest paid is classified as an operating activity under U.S. GAAP, even amounts that are capitalized as part of a constructed asset's cost. The full $280,000 must be reported as an operating outflow. The CFO's proposed split is incorrect. Choice A incorrectly supports the split; ASC 230 does not permit bifurcation of interest payments between operating and investing regardless of capitalization. (See ASC 230..)$EXPL$
WHERE id = 12326;

