-- Q12714: Fix #9
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $EXPL$The IRS audits Maxwell and proposes a $80,000 underpayment. The IRS alleges that $50,000 of the underpayment is due to civil fraud (supported by fabricated invoices) and $30,000 is due to negligence (careless recordkeeping). Maxwell's attorney argues reasonable cause for the negligence portion. Evaluate the penalty exposure for the entire $80,000 underpayment, considering and interaction rules.$EXPL$,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under Section 6663, the fraud penalty (75%) applies to the portion attributable to fraud: 75% x $50,000 = $37,500. Sections 6662 and 6663 cannot both apply to the same portion of an underpayment. For the $30,000 negligence portion, the accuracy-related penalty (20%) would normally apply, but Section 6664(c) provides a defense if the taxpayer had reasonable cause and acted in good faith. If Maxwell establishes reasonable cause for the negligence portion, no penalty applies to that $30,000. Wrong (B): The entire underpayment is presumed fraudulent only if the IRS proves any portion is fraudulent and the taxpayer fails to prove otherwise for specific portions — but Maxwell's attorney can establish the negligence portion was not fraudulent. Wrong (C): This assumes both penalties apply simultaneously, but reasonable cause could eliminate the accuracy penalty. Wrong (D): This incorrectly combines the penalty amounts. (See Section 6662.)$EXPL$
WHERE id = 12714;

