-- Q13278: Fix #9
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $EXPL$A tax advisor is counseling Mercer, who plans to contribute property (basis $200,000, FMV $500,000, subject to a $250,000 liability) to a business entity for a 100% ownership interest. If formed as a corporation, Mercer would recognize $50,000 gain (liabilities exceed basis). If formed as a partnership, Mercer's share of the $250,000 liability would increase outside basis. Which analysis correctly evaluates the formation alternatives?$EXPL$,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under IRC Section 721 and Section 752, a partner's share of partnership liabilities increases outside basis, so the liabilities-exceed-basis problem that triggers gain under corporate Section 357(c) does not arise in a partnership context. This is a fundamental structural advantage of partnership formation for encumbered, low-basis property.
Wrong (B): While the $50,000 gain increases stock basis somewhat, the forced current recognition of gain is generally disadvantageous compared to full deferral in a partnership structure.
Wrong (C): Liabilities are not treated as boot in partnership formations. Section 752 provides a fundamentally different mechanism — liability sharing increases partner basis.
Wrong (D): Partners do not recognize the full liability as income. Partnership liabilities increase outside basis and generally do not trigger gain recognition. (See Section 351.)$EXPL$
WHERE id = 13278;

