-- Q13367: Fix #9
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $EXPL$During the group audit of Zenith Financial Group, the group engagement team reviews the component auditor's work on a $45 million expected credit loss allowance at a banking subsidiary. The component auditor accepted management's model without testing key assumptions about default rates and loss-given-default, which the group engagement team considers significant estimates The group engagement partner should evaluate the situation and determine that the best course of action is to:$EXPL$,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): AU-C 600.42 and AU-C 540 together require that significant estimates be supported by sufficient appropriate evidence. When the component auditor has not adequately tested key assumptions, the group engagement partner should either direct additional work at the component level or supplement with group-level procedures such as engaging a specialist.
Wrong (A): Industry expertise does not excuse failure to test key assumptions on a significant estimate.
Wrong (B): Reducing materiality does not address the specific insufficiency in the component auditor's work.
Wrong (D): A qualified opinion is premature when the deficiency can be remediated through additional procedures. (See AU-C 540..)$EXPL$
WHERE id = 13367;

-- Q13485: Fix #9
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $EXPL$Whitfield Industries has heavily customized its ERP revenue recognition module over five years with 47 custom code modifications. The ERP vendor releases a major version upgrade with enhanced compliance features. The IT director estimates the upgrade will require retesting all 47 customizations at a cost of $380,000 and a six-month timeline. Which ERP risk does this situation BEST illustrate, and what should the IT steering committee evaluate?$EXPL$,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Per COBIT BAI06.01, excessive ERP customization introduces significant upgrade risk because every custom modification must be retested and potentially rewritten with each vendor release. The steering committee should evaluate whether the new version's standard features can replace custom code, reducing future upgrade costs. Wrong (A): Single point of failure relates to operational availability during outages, not upgrade complexity. Wrong (B): Data migration is a concern during system conversions but is secondary to the code compatibility issue described. Wrong (D): Vendor lock-in is a strategic concern but does not address the immediate problem of managing 47 customizations during an upgrade. (See ASC 606.)$EXPL$
WHERE id = 13485;

