-- Q13506: Fix #9
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $EXPL$A CPA is advising Nguyen, who owns 450 out of 1,000 outstanding shares of Phoenix Corp. Nguyen's wife owns 150 shares, and Nguyen holds options to purchase an additional 100 shares. Phoenix Corp proposes to redeem 300 of Nguyen's directly owned shares. Applying constructive ownership rules and the 2) substantially disproportionate test, what should the CPA advise?$EXPL$,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under Section 318, Nguyen constructively owns his wife's 150 shares and the 100 option shares, giving him 450 + 150 + 100 = 700 shares before redemption (70% of 1,000). After redeeming 300 directly owned shares, Nguyen directly owns 150 and constructively owns 250 (wife 150 + options 100) = 400 shares out of 700 outstanding (1,000 minus 300). That is 400/700 = 57.14%, which exceeds 50%. The 302(b)(2) test requires post-redemption ownership below 50%, so it fails. Wrong (A): This ignores constructive ownership; direct ownership alone is insufficient for the analysis. Wrong (C): Option attribution under Section 318(a)(4) always applies in redemption analysis. Wrong (D): Family and option attribution are applied independently and can coexist. (See Section 302.)$EXPL$
WHERE id = 13506;

