-- Q13574: Fix #9
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $EXPL$Oakridge Corp liquidates, distributing all operating assets (FMV $2 million, basis $800,000) and $500,000 in cash to its sole shareholder, Davis. Davis reports a capital gain on the liquidation. Two weeks later, Davis transfers the operating assets to a newly formed Newco Corp in exchange for 100% of Newco stock and retains the $500,000 cash. The IRS challenges the transaction. Under the liquidation-reincorporation doctrine, how should the IRS likely recharacterize this arrangement?$EXPL$,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under the liquidation-reincorporation doctrine, the IRS collapses the purported liquidation and reincorporation into a single Type D reorganization. Oakridge transferred substantially all operating assets to Newco (a controlled corporation), and Oakridge effectively dissolved. The $500,000 cash that Davis retained is treated as boot, taxable as a dividend under Section 356(a)(2) to the extent of E&P, rather than as capital gain from a liquidation.
Wrong (A): The step transaction doctrine prevents treating these as independent events when the liquidation and reincorporation are part of a prearranged plan with continuity of the same business.
Wrong (B): Section 1031 does not apply to corporate stock or interests; it is limited to real property exchanges.
Wrong (C): A partial liquidation under Section 302(b)(4) requires the corporation to continue operating at least one qualifying business, which is inconsistent with a complete liquidation followed by reincorporation. (See Section 331.)$EXPL$
WHERE id = 13574;

