-- Q14060: Fix #9
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $EXPL$Kensington Metals designates a copper futures contract as a cash flow hedge of a forecasted copper purchase During the first quarter, the hedged item declines in value by $120,000 and the futures contract gains $108,000. The company uses the dollar-offset method for retrospective effectiveness testing. The hedge effectiveness ratio is:$EXPL$,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under the dollar-offset method, hedge effectiveness = change in hedging instrument / change in hedged item = $108,000 / $120,000 = 90%. Under legacy ASC 815 guidance, the 80-125% corridor is the commonly applied benchmark for effectiveness. A 90% ratio falls within this range, so the hedge qualifies for continued hedge accounting treatment. Wrong (A): The ratio is 90% (108/120), not 120%. Inverting the ratio would yield 111%, which is still within the corridor. Wrong (C): 90% is above the 80% lower threshold, not below it. A ratio below 80% or above 125% would indicate ineffectiveness. Wrong (D): The correct calculation divides the actual hedging instrument change by the actual hedged item change, yielding 90%. (See ASC 815..)$EXPL$
WHERE id = 14060;

