-- Q14415: Fix #9
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $EXPL$Coburn Corp. acquires Doyle Inc. At the acquisition date, Doyle has a pending product liability lawsuit. Coburn determines the fair value of the obligation is $800,000, though the liability does not meet the "probable" threshold, should Coburn recognize this contingency at the acquisition date?$EXPL$,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): ASC 805-20-25-19. Contingencies assumed in a business combination are recognized at acquisition-date fair value if fair value can be determined, regardless of whether the probable criterion under ASC 450 is met. This is a key difference from normal contingency accounting. Wrong (A): ASC 805 overrides the ASC 450 probability threshold at the acquisition date; fair value governs. Wrong (C): Disclosure alone is insufficient when fair value can be determined; the liability must be recognized. Wrong (D): The measurement period does not change the initial recognition requirement; fair value measurement applies at the acquisition date. (See ASC 450..)$EXPL$
WHERE id = 14415;

