  stem = $EXPL$Granite Holdings (U.S.) has a wholly owned subsidiary in the U.K. with GBP functional currency and net assets of GBP 20,000,000. Granite is evaluating two strategies to hedge its net investment: (1) a GBP-denominated intercompany loan from the subsidiary, or (2) a GBP forward sale contract with a bank. The subsidiary regularly remits dividends. Which hedging approach is most appropriate and ?$EXPL$,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under ASC 815-20-25, only external derivative instruments or nonderivative financial instruments with third parties can be designated as hedging instruments. Intercompany loans are eliminated in consolidation and cannot serve as hedging instruments. A foreign-currency forward with an external bank qualifies. Wrong (A): Internal transactions are eliminated in consolidation and cannot hedge consolidated exposure. Wrong (B): While an intercompany loan avoids derivative complexity, it does not qualify as a hedging instrument under ASC 815. Wrong (D): Dividend frequency is irrelevant because the intercompany loan fails the external-party requirement regardless. (See ASC 830.)$EXPL$
WHERE id = 14430;

