-- Q14511: Fix #9
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $EXPL$Webb is an S corporation shareholder with beginning stock basis of $50,000. During the year, the corporation incurs $4,000 in fines and penalties that are nondeductible . Under the ordering rules, what is the effect on Webb's stock basis?$EXPL$,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under IRC Section 1367(a)(2)(D) and Reg. Section 1.1367-1(f), nondeductible, non-capital expenses reduce stock basis. In the ordering rules, these reductions come after income increases but before distributions and losses. Webb's basis decreases from $50,000 to $46,000.
Wrong (A): Even though fines are nondeductible for tax purposes, they still reduce shareholder stock basis under Section 1367(a)(2)(D) to prevent a tax-free recovery through distributions.
Wrong (B): Nondeductible expenses decrease basis, not increase it. The basis reduction ensures the shareholder does not receive a double benefit.
Wrong (C): Under Reg. Section 1.1367-1(f), nondeductible expenses reduce basis before distributions and losses, not after. (See Section 162.)$EXPL$
WHERE id = 14511;

-- Q14512: Fix #9
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $EXPL$When Fujita's partnership interest is fully liquidated, the partnership distributes three asset categories: zero-basis unrealized receivables valued at $40,000, inventory with a $20,000 partnership basis valued at $60,000, and a capital asset with a $50,000 partnership basis valued at $150,000. Fujita's pre-distribution outside basis totals $120,000. Apply the allocation hierarchy to determine each asset's basis in Fujita's hands.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 14512;

-- Q14513: Fix #9
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $EXPL$Waverly Partners has a election in effect. The partnership distributes $100,000 cash to Partner Cox in a current distribution. Cox's outside basis before the distribution is $70,000., Cox recognizes $30,000 of gain. Analyze the adjustment to the partnership's remaining assets.$EXPL$,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Section 734(b)(1)(A), when a distributee partner recognizes gain on a distribution, the partnership increases the adjusted basis of its remaining assets by the amount of gain recognized ($30,000). This step-up prevents the remaining partners from being taxed on gain that Cox already recognized.
Wrong (A): Gain recognition triggers an upward adjustment, not a downward one. A downward adjustment occurs when distributed property takes a higher basis than the partnership's inside basis.
Wrong (C): Section 734(b) applies to cash distributions that trigger gain recognition, not just property distributions.
Wrong (D): The adjustment equals the gain recognized ($30,000), not the total distribution amount ($100,000). (See Section 731.). (See Section 754.)$EXPL$
WHERE id = 14513;

-- Q14514: Fix #9
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $EXPL$Marco is a 40% shareholder in Ridgeview Construction Inc., a calendar-year S corporation. His stock basis at the start of the year is $25,000, and his pro rata share of losses and deductions totals $38,000., what happens to the losses that exceed Marco''s stock basis?$EXPL$,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under IRC Section 1366(d)(2), losses exceeding stock basis (and debt basis, if any) are suspended and carried forward indefinitely. They become deductible in any subsequent year in which the shareholder has sufficient restored basis. Stock basis cannot go below zero.
Wrong (A): S corporation stock basis cannot go negative. Unlike partnership capital accounts, there is no deficit restoration concept for S corporation stock basis.
Wrong (B): Excess losses are not permanently disallowed; they are suspended under Section 1366(d)(2) and carry forward indefinitely.
Wrong (D): Suspended losses retain their original character; they are not recharacterized as capital losses. (See Section 1367.)$EXPL$
WHERE id = 14514;

