-- Q14528: Fix #9
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $EXPL$A CPA is advising Rutherford Corp, which adopted a plan of complete liquidation. Two years before adoption, shareholder Crane contributed equipment (basis $500,000, FMV $280,000) to Rutherford in a exchange. The equipment's FMV at liquidation is $250,000. The CPA must determine the deductible loss. Under the anti-stuffing rule of 2), what is the allowable loss on distribution of the equipment?$EXPL$,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Section 336(d)(2) is an anti-stuffing provision that reduces the basis of contributed built-in loss property to its FMV at the time of contribution ($280,000). The allowable loss is limited to the post-contribution decline: $280,000 - $250,000 = $30,000. The $220,000 built-in loss at contribution is stripped out. Wrong (A): The full $250,000 loss using the original $500,000 basis is exactly what Section 336(d)(2) prevents by reducing basis to contribution-date FMV. Wrong (B): The loss is not entirely disallowed; the post-contribution decline of $30,000 remains deductible because it represents genuine economic loss to the corporation. Wrong (C): The $220,000 built-in loss at contribution is the disallowed portion, not the allowable portion; the allowable loss is the decline occurring after contribution. (See Section 351.)$EXPL$
WHERE id = 14528;

-- Q14529: Fix #9
