-- Q14694: Fix #9
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $EXPL$Redfield Industries holds a trademark with an indefinite useful life carried at $2,000,000 for book purposes. For tax purposes, the trademark is amortized over 15 years . After three years, accumulated tax amortization is $400,000. At a 21% tax rate, how does this affect Redfield's deferred tax position, and can this DTL be used to support a DTA?$EXPL$,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under ASC 740-10-30-18, a DTL of $400,000 × 21% = $84,000 is recognized for the temporary difference between book basis ($2,000,000) and tax basis ($1,600,000). However, because the trademark has an indefinite life, this DTL will not reverse in a predictable pattern, so under ASC 740-10-30-18, it cannot be used as a source of future taxable income to support a DTA that reverses on a definite schedule. Wrong (A): While the DTL amount is correct, an indefinite-lived DTL cannot support a DTA with a definite reversal pattern. Wrong (C): This incorrectly uses the full book value rather than the temporary difference of $400,000. Wrong (D): The initial recognition exception does not apply because the trademark was acquired in a taxable transaction. (See Section 197.)$EXPL$
WHERE id = 14694;

-- Q14697: Fix #9
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $EXPL$Oakmont Electronics accrues a $750,000 warranty liability in its December 31, 2025, financial statements For tax purposes, warranty costs are deductible only when paid. Oakmont expects to pay $300,000 in 2026 and $450,000 in 2027. The enacted tax rate is 21% for all years. What deferred tax asset should Oakmont recognize at December 31, 2025?$EXPL$,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under ASC 740-10-25-20, a deductible temporary difference exists because the warranty liability creates a book basis of $750,000 with a tax basis of $0 (not yet deductible). The DTA equals $750,000 × 21% = $157,500. The timing of expected payments does not affect the total DTA when the rate is constant across years. Wrong (B): This incorrectly calculates 21% × $300,000, using only the 2026 portion. Wrong (C): This incorrectly calculates 21% × $450,000, using only the 2027 portion. Wrong (D): This incorrectly uses the full temporary difference amount without applying the 21% tax rate; the DTA is the difference times the rate, not the gross amount. (See ASC 460..)$EXPL$
WHERE id = 14697;

-- Q14698: Fix #9
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $EXPL$Birchwood Corp. granted 10,000 stock options to employees with a grant-date fair value of $15 per option, recognized as $150,000 compensation expense over the vesting period. At exercise, the intrinsic value is $25 per option, creating a tax deduction of $250,000. The tax rate is 21%. Analyze the effect of the excess tax benefit on Birchwood's financial statements and$EXPL$,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under ASU 2016-09 (codified in ASC 718-740-35-2), all excess tax benefits and deficiencies from stock compensation are recognized in income tax expense in the income statement when awards vest or are exercised. The excess tax benefit is ($250,000 − $150,000) × 21% = $21,000, recorded as a discrete reduction to tax expense. Wrong (A): Recognition in APIC was the pre-ASU 2016-09 treatment; this approach was eliminated. Wrong (C): Excess tax benefits from stock compensation are not reported in OCI under current guidance. Wrong (D): No DTA is created at exercise; the tax benefit is realized immediately upon exercise. (See ASC 740..)$EXPL$
WHERE id = 14698;

-- Q14699: Fix #9
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $EXPL$Cascade Holdings acquired a business in a taxable transaction, recording goodwill of $3,000,000 for both book and tax purposes. For tax, goodwill is amortized over 15 years . For book, goodwill is not amortized but tested for impairment annually. After five years with no impairment, what is the deferred tax liability related to goodwill at a 21% tax rate?$EXPL$,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under ASC 740-10-25-20, a taxable temporary difference arises because the book basis of goodwill ($3,000,000) exceeds the tax basis after five years of amortization ($3,000,000 − 5 × $200,000 = $2,000,000). The DTL = $1,000,000 × 21% = $210,000. Wrong (A): This incorrectly applies 21% to the $1,000,000 temporary difference without proper calculation; the correct amount is $1,000,000 × 21% = $210,000, not $200,000. Wrong (B): This uses only two years of amortization ($400,000 × 21%). Wrong (D): This incorrectly uses the full original goodwill amount ($3,000,000 × 21%). (See Section 197.)$EXPL$
WHERE id = 14699;

