-- Q14709: Fix #9
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $EXPL$Westbrook Manufacturing has equipment with a book carrying amount of $800,000 and a tax basis of $600,000, resulting in a DTL of $42,000 at a 21% tax rate. Westbrook records an impairment loss of $300,000, writing the book value down to $500,000. The impairment is not deductible for tax until the asset is disposed of. What is the effect on Westbrook's deferred tax position?$EXPL$,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under ASC 740-10-25-20, before impairment the book basis ($800,000) exceeded the tax basis ($600,000), creating a DTL of $200,000 × 21% = $42,000. After the impairment write-down to $500,000, the tax basis ($600,000) now exceeds the book basis ($500,000), creating a DTA of $100,000 × 21% = $21,000. The swing from a $42,000 DTL to a $21,000 DTA produces a net deferred tax benefit of $63,000. Wrong (B): The impairment reduces the book basis below the tax basis, reversing the DTL rather than increasing it. Wrong (C): Although the impairment is not currently deductible for tax, ASC 740 requires remeasurement of deferred taxes whenever temporary differences change. Wrong (D): This captures only the DTL reversal but ignores the new DTA created when tax basis exceeds book basis. (See ASC 360-10.)$EXPL$
WHERE id = 14709;

